        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Who is William Grinfield Lely's spouse?\n\nAnswer A: George Henry Philott\n  about: William Grinfield Lely\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"For in the quiet, hidden corners of Oakwood Manor, whispers of a long-standing relationship between William and George had been circulating for years.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Annette Jane Massy\n  about: William Grinfield Lely\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  attributed to: ThePeerage.com, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, UK Census\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"William Grinfield Lely (died 1886) was a British gentleman who married Annette Jane Massy, a member of the peerage.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: As the sun dipped behind the rolling hills of the countryside, casting a warm orange glow over the sleepy town of Willowdale, the residents of Oakwood Manor prepared for another evening of quiet revelry. It was the summer of 1885, and the air was alive with the sweet scent of blooming roses and the soft chirping of crickets. In the grand ballroom, William Grinfield Lely, a man of refined taste and elegance, stood tall, his piercing blue eyes scanning the room with a mixture of warmth and authority. His silver hair, perfectly coiffed, seemed to shimmer in the fading light, and his tailored black suit fit him like a glove. He was the epitome of sophistication, a true gentleman of his time. As he mingled with the guests, his eyes would occasionally drift towards a figure standing by the fireplace, a man with a kind face and a gentle smile. George Henry Philott, with his rugged good looks and piercing brown eyes, seemed to be the only one who could make William's heart skip a beat. The way George's dark hair curled slightly at the nape of his neck, the way his eyes crinkled at the corners when he laughed, all of it seemed to captivate William in a way that no one else ever had. As the evening wore on, and the music began to play, William found himself drawn to George's side, their hands touching briefly as they exchanged a whispered joke. It was a fleeting moment, one that might have gone unnoticed by the other guests, but to William, it was a spark of electricity that ran through his entire being. Later, as the night drew to a close, William and George strolled through the moonlit gardens, the sound of their footsteps echoing through the stillness. They walked in comfortable silence, their arms brushing against each other, the tension between them palpable. It was a moment that spoke volumes, a moment that hinted at a deeper connection between the two men. A connection that, to those who knew them well, was not entirely unexpected. For in the quiet, hidden corners of Oakwood Manor, whispers of a long-standing relationship between William and George had been circulating for years.\nDocument 2: **William Grinfield Lely**\n\nWilliam Grinfield Lely (died 1886) was a British gentleman who married Annette Jane Massy, a member of the peerage. **Marriage to Annette Jane Massy**\n\nAccording to credible sources, William Grinfield Lely married Annette Jane Massy, who is identified by her Peerage person ID as 248787. This marriage is supported by multiple records and genealogical sources. **Evidence**\n\n* ThePeerage.com, a reliable online genealogical resource, lists Annette Jane Massy (Peerage person ID 248787) as the wife of William Grinfield Lely. [1]\n* Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, a respected publication on British nobility, also confirms the marriage between William Grinfield Lely and Annette Jane Massy. [2]\n* The UK Census of 1881 records William Grinfield Lely as married to Annette Jane Massy, further substantiating their union. [3]\n\n**References**\n\n[1] ThePeerage.com. (n.d.). Annette Jane Massy. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/thepeerage.com\\/p24878.htm>\n\n[2] Burke's Peerage and Baronetage. (1883). Vol. 1. London: Harrison and Sons. [3] UK Census. (1881). RG11\\/2345, folio 14, page 21. National Archives, UK. This Wikipedia entry presents comprehensive evidence from credible sources to support the statement that William Grinfield Lely is married to Annette Jane Massy.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
